omething that's only  become possible with the rapid  development of computers but if all the  data is in form of computer and storage  available we can send the data of the  picture  by the Internet worldwide without any  borders so does it mean that our views  for instance can send you and their  x-ray pictures and you take a look at it  and make a diagnosis yeah well  theoretically yes of course it is very  easy to send these images around the  world provided they're not too large and  this is very useful for a second opinion  for example however it's very important  that not only we take the pictures but  we interpret them with the patient with  knowledge about the patient so we need  to know what computer patients  complaints are we need to know what may  be other examination results are and  then we put this all together into a  report which hopefully eventually gives  the correct diagnosis of this patient  sorry still doctors and you do the  examination as well absolutely radiology  is not all about x-rays and making  diagnosis you can even treat patients  with something called interventional  radiology what is that well  interventional radiology really started  off as part of angiography and geography  is a technique where we insert tiny  little catheters in two patients  arteries via the groin and then we  inject contrast and make x-rays of the  arteries and this is something we've  done for decades and some smart  radiologists started thinking about the  option of not just using this for  diagnosis but for actually using the  catheters in the arteries for treatment  and those monitors you can see a patient  very actually closed up an artery so  what happened there this is a patient  and who had bowel cancer in the past and  has recently developed metastases to the  liver he was operated on those  metastases once but then one of the  metastases came back which we can see  here on the image so what we did was we  interrupted the blood supply to the  liver as